insert into public.day_recaps (series_id, season_number, week, day, recap) values
('dark-moon', 1, 4, 1, 'When a lamp was lit again under the Kiln, the captive was gone. Somebody had stood within arm''s reach of you in the dark with a blade, and used it only on his ropes. Garrick, still at your shoulder, said, “Every time they''ve had knives out at you, not one of you has died.” You walked Wren home across the ridges to Ashcombe, and the ledger''s last name sent you on: Joss, of Reedham, a fishing village on the Stillwater two days north, already in their hands. Why are you still alive? The north road, at first light.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 4, 2, 'On the north road three hungry men tried you at a ford and lost. They told you what they had let go by the day before: four armed riders with a little closed cart, whose tracks turned away from the lake road and the boy. A carter at the fork paid you in an old coin with the king''s face scraped off, and said he had watched them pass. “One box, no bigger than a bread crock. Four armed men for one little box.” You had seen a small box before, held to a robed man''s chest as he climbed out of the Kiln, so what is in it? Reedham, by noon tomorrow.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 4, 3, 'Reedham sits on the Stillwater, and a stone''s throw out a bell tower stands up out of the lake, the last of the drowned old village. Joss''s grandmother said they asked for him by name and knew the night he was born, and her doorpost held eight fresh notches, one for every night he has been gone. They needed Wren only the once and still kept her two weeks, so they are waiting for something. You rowed her leaking skiff out to the tower Meira marked, and found the old mark there with a cold fragment beside it. Back on the shore were fresh keel-marks that were not hers: someone has been rowing out to the drowned village by night. Old Reedham, at the shallow end, tomorrow.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 4, 4, 'In the lowest lane of Old Reedham, the drowned streets a dry year had left above the lake, you found men''s boot-prints a few days old with smaller ones between them. They ended at the deep water. Joss was walked through there alive, and taken on by boat. That evening you found the day''s fragment already copied into Meira''s notes, beside her line: “‘Moon-blood’ — the old songs'' word for the marked.” The word takes in Tamsin, Corin, Wren, Joss and you, and the Seekers left a warning with it. A warning against what? The reed island, a mile out, is the only place on this lake a boat can put in unseen.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 4, 5, 'You rowed through thick white fog to the reed island. On its far side a man minding a boat with a small black circle on its prow fought you, then slipped away into the reeds and left the boat to you. In its bottom lay fresh curls of whittled wood and a tiny split hull, thrown away, so Joss is alive and still carving. When the fog lifted you saw a lamp low on the black water, moving away toward the drowned tower. Why would anyone keep watch on an empty tower? After that lamp, tonight, in their own boat.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 4, 6, 'You rowed after the lamp in the Order''s own boat, but it was out long before you reached the tower, and nobody was there. The sixth fragment lay on the stair, and in the bell chamber the six became a map once more, read aloud by the light of one struggling flame. It led under Old Reedham, down the stair of the drowned church, to a room below the streets where the lake stands knee-deep. When the flame blew out, wood knocked on wood right beneath the tower, and oars pulled hard away toward the drowned village: somebody had heard every word. Down the stair, into their boat, and after that sound, now.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 4, 7, 'You followed the oars across the black lake to the drowned church, and down its stair to an undercroft where the water stood knee-deep. Joss lay on a stone table, alive, his cut palm still bleeding through its linen and a little wooden boat in his good hand. Behind him the socket was empty, and the dust at its rim had not settled. They had reached it barely ahead of you, the same night your map gave it up, and beyond the water-gate a second boat with a black circle on its prow pulled away. Their Second Book said marked blood set on a shard of the broken moon wakes it, and that the faithful follow the Seekers'' children to every way only their blood can open. Every empty socket had held a Stone, carried home to him through a door you opened. It was never the children''s blood alone. The blood wakes the Stones, and the Stones wake him. Garrick spoke the king''s name like a man who has always known it. The captive carried Meira''s pen, and Meira is dead. A torn page said a fifth Stone remembers whose blood, and broke off at “the fifth shall end—”. Coming out, you saw the dawn stop halfway, and from far to the north came a sound like a heartbeat.')
